  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1\n\nIt will be starting off quite cold early this morning with areas of \nfrost around. Skies are clear and temperatures will be in the upper \n20s to 30s for the valleys, with low 40s across the hilltops. Frost \nAdvisories remain in effect until 9 AM for all of Northeast PA; \nwhile the urban valley locations of Scranton and Wilkes-Barre may \nnot see frost, immediate surrounding locations likely will. There \ncould also be patchy frost in some of the colder pockets surrounding \nthe Finger Lakes, but coverage is not widespread enough for \nadvisories at this time. Some patchy river valley fog will also be \naround through mid morning. After that, temperatures warm up into \nthe 60s this afternoon under mostly sunny skies. There will be a few \nmore clouds around across the Syracuse metro to I-90 corridor this \nafternoon as a weak frontal disturbance swings through. Additional \nshowers will develop overnight into Thursday off of Lake America in \nthe cool westerly flow. 850mb temperatures drop to around +4C on \nThursday, giving enough lake induced instability for the shower \ndevelopment. With the increased cloud cover it won't be nearly as \ncool tonight with lows only dipping down into the mid-40s to low \n50s. Thursday will still be mild with highs in the 60s. Breezy \nnorthwest winds are expected to develop through the day on Thursday, \nas deep boundary layer mixing is anticipated up to the 800mb level. \nWinds were increased from the blended guidance, and surface winds \nwill likely be between 10-20 mph with a few higher gusts (25-30 mph).\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2\n\nHigh pressure returns on Friday, with just a few lingering lake \neffect clouds and perhaps a lingering shower across northern Oneida \nCounty. Otherwise, expect increasing sunshine and seasonable \ntemperatures. The surface high slides east of the area during the \nday on Saturday, with a southerly return flow developing. This will \nboost temperatures up into the mid-60s to low 70s with plenty of \nsunshine expected.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3\n\nWe will be keeping a close eye on TD-9, which is expected to \nstrengthen over the Gulf in the coming days. This system and its \nremnants are then forecast to move north into the Ohio Valley later \nin the weekend into early next week. The exact track and timing \nremain uncertain. However, the latest model guidance is coming into \nbetter agreement that rain will overspread our area, likely sometime \nSunday into Monday depending on exact system movement and speed. The \nensemble guidance is increasing PoPs up into the likely and even \ncategorical range late Sunday into early Monday as PWATs potential \nsurge out ahead of the incoming system.",
